---
/**
 * Where a submission lands.
 *
 * Somebody has just spent ten minutes writing carefully, pressed a button, and watched
 * everything they wrote disappear from the screen. The job of this page is to make that
 * feel like an arrival rather than a loss: say what happened, say what happens next, say
 * when, and say what to do if they want to change something.
 *
 * What happened changed on 2026-08-18: it is published. This page used to be about a wait
 * — "expect a few days, not minutes" — and the wait is gone. The temptation now is to skip
 * the page and drop somebody straight onto their report, which would be wrong for the same
 * reason it was wrong before: the two things they most need to know are that the text is
 * now fixed, and that the most useful thing they could do next is post a failure.
 */
import Account from '../../layouts/Account.astro';
import { path } from '../../lib/paths';
import { LICENCE } from '../../lib/site';
---

<Account
  title="Published"
  description="What happens to a report once it is posted."
  lead="It is in the corpus. Nothing else is needed from you."
  gate="required"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What happens next</h2>

    <ul class="tombstone-list">
      <li>
        It is readable now. Nobody approves a report before it appears — the corpus is a
        record of what people did, not a set of accounts somebody vouched for.
      </li>
      <li>
        It appears in the listings within a minute, and on its own page after the next
        nightly build. Both show an open tombstone until somebody confirms it still works.
      </li>
      <li>
        If a reader thinks something is wrong with it, they can flag it. A moderator then
        decides whether it stays up, and writes you the reason either way. You will find
        that under <a href={path('/account/#decisions')}>moderation decisions</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Published accounts are licensed <a href={LICENCE.url} rel="license">{LICENCE.name}</a>.
        You keep copyright and are credited under whatever name your profile shows.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you want to change it</h2>

    <p>
      The text is fixed, and that is deliberate rather than an oversight: other people
      confirm that a report still works, and those confirmations would otherwise attest to a
      version nobody can read any more. To correct something, post a comment on your own
      report — the thread is part of the record, and a correction in it is more useful to a
      reader than a silent edit would have been.
    </p>

    <p>
      Deleting your own report is always possible, at any time, from its page. If something
      more serious is wrong — third-party material you meant to remove, above all — flag it
      yourself and say so. A moderator can hide it, and a hidden report is editable by its
      author.
    </p>

    <h2>The most useful thing you could do now</h2>

    <p>
      Post a second one about something that <em>did not</em> work. Accounts of failure are
      the ones nobody publishes and the ones this corpus is short of — and they are usually
      quicker to write, because you already know where it went wrong.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="button button--primary" href={path('/reports/new/')}>Post another</a>
    <a class="button" href={path('/account/')}>Back to your profile</a>
  </div>
</Account>
